  "id": "pujllay_ayarichi_yampara",
  "name": "Pujllay and Ayarichi (Yampara music of Tarabuco)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Music and dance of the Yampara culture around Tarabuco in Chuquisaca, Bolivia, on UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. Pujllay belongs to the rainy season and Carnival and celebrates the renewal of life brought by the rains: men dance in sandals fitted with large metal spurs while musicians play flutes and a horn clarinet. Ayarichi belongs to the dry season and the saints' feasts: four male dancer-musicians play panpipes and drums at once, with two to four young women who dance and sing. The flute card stands in for the Pujllay flutes and the horns card for the horn clarinet.",
  "recipe": "classic modern pujllay and ayarichi, carnival Processional, andean branch. andean tradition paired rows interlocking siku panpipes, flute like credited flute credited horns unspecified, acoustic membrane drum. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
